  "notes": "The big-room prodigy who migrated to progressive house and future bass. The single most useful documented fact is a production one: the kick is \"pre-pitched — the documented practice is choosing from twelve chromatically-pitched kick samples rather than transposing one\" — and \"because the kick is pitched, kick and bass are effectively one instrument\". That is encoded as a bass locked to the kick at 0.97 roots in the lowest register in this section, 24-43, so the two occupy the same octave and read as one voice. The rest of the big-room grammar is the entry's too: \"minimal percussion, clap on 2&4, 16th hats, and long build-ups into an electro-style drop; often only a kick or tom and a couple of hi-hats in the drop\", which is a tom/riser/impact lane at 0-2 hits a bar and the lowest open-hat probability among the four-on-the-floor producers here. Melodies stay \"simple and minimal\" per the big-room convention, so the note density is low and the families are few. The lead is \"built by layering synths by function — a clean mono synth for definition, a detuned stereo saw for width\" with the discipline of muting each layer to test whether it earns its place, which is the octave-doubling countermelody rather than an independent line. He produces in FL Studio. ⚠ The tempo comes from the big-room convention by the entry's own statement, and the velocities, densities and registers are INFERRED.",
